Description: The stereo declaration follows the Vulkan swapchain current on the surface
 A surface whose two-view swapchain was replaced by a one-view one kept its
 declaration of a pair, so the compositor showed the one picture as two
 halves, one per eye. The Vulkan stereo layer declared a Wayland surface or
 an X11 window when it made a two-layer swapchain and withdrew only when
 the surface was destroyed; Mesa's own X11 presentation never withdrew.
 .
 Measured with a client that presents two layers, then one, then two on one
 surface: through the layer on NVIDIA's driver each eye showed one half of
 the one-layer picture on Wayland; on X11, through the layer and through
 radv's and lavapipe's own presentation, the one-layer picture was mixed
 with the last pair and the window still declared two views. Mesa's own
 Wayland presentation was right: it destroys its declaration with the
 swapchain, and the retired one's when a new swapchain replaces it.
 .
 The layer now withdraws its declaration when a swapchain it does not pack
 is made on the surface, and when its pair swapchain that is current there
 is destroyed with nothing after it. A pair retired by a newer swapchain
 ("Upon calling vkCreateSwapchainKHR with an oldSwapchain that is not
 VK_NULL_HANDLE, oldSwapchain is retired", VkSwapchainCreateInfoKHR) leaves
 the declaration as the newer one has it. On Wayland the withdrawal takes
 effect with the surface's next commit, the first picture of what comes
 next; on X11 the window shows one picture from its next frame on. A
 surface is destroyed after its swapchains
 (VUID-vkDestroySurfaceKHR-surface-01266); one destroyed before is no
 longer looked at.
 .
 Mesa's X11 presentation follows the same rule for the window, and its
 surface's stereo fields start initialised: vk_alloc2 does not clear them.
 The X11 withdrawal is one helper, shared with the take-back of a pair the
 X server copies.
Author: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Forwarded: not-needed, the stereo declaration and the Vulkan stereo layer are the edition's own
Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5
